PERCONA: keep _ssl/_hashlib at OpenSSL 3.0 versioned symbols.

Built against OpenSSL >= 3.3 headers (Rocky 9.8 ships 3.5), two CPython
extension modules silently pick up versioned symbol needs newer than
3.0.0, so they fail to load on OpenSSL 3.0 hosts (Ubuntu 22.04/24.04,
Debian 12) whenever the HOST libcrypto/libssl is already mapped and
soname dedup wins over the tarball's bundled copies — exactly what
happens inside a postgres backend using plpython3u:

 - Modules/_ssl.c: upstream carries a lock-and-deep-copy polyfill of
   X509_STORE_get1_objects (gh-114572) but compiles it only for
   OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER < 3.3, taking the library function (symbol
   version OPENSSL_3.3.0) on newer headers. Compile the polyfill
   unconditionally, renamed so it cannot clash with the library
   declaration on >= 3.3 headers. The polyfill is upstream's own code
   with the same semantics as the library function.

 - Modules/_hashopenssl.c: OpenSSL 3.4 redefines the EVP_MD_CTX_size()
   compatibility macro to call EVP_MD_CTX_get_size_ex(), a function
   introduced in 3.4.0 (symbol version OPENSSL_3.4.0). Restore the
   3.0-era expansion (identical behavior for the fixed-size digests
   CPython queries). Same fix as our pgcrypto OpenSSL-3.4 patch.

Both changes are inert when building against OpenSSL 1.1.1 headers
(EL8): the _ssl polyfill was already compiled there (only its static
symbol name changes) and the _hashopenssl remap is version-guarded.
Upstream note: gh-121249 tracks the same class of problem; nothing to
flag upstream — this is a distribution choice to build on new headers
while staying loadable on 3.0 hosts, which upstream binaries make by
building against older OpenSSL instead.

--- a/Modules/_hashopenssl.c
+++ b/Modules/_hashopenssl.c
@@ -37,6 +37,17 @@
 #include <openssl/objects.h>
 #include <openssl/err.h>
 
+/*
+ * PERCONA: OpenSSL 3.4 remaps EVP_MD_CTX_size() to
+ * EVP_MD_CTX_get_size_ex() (OPENSSL_3.4.0 symbol version). Use the
+ * 3.0-era expansion so the module stays loadable on OpenSSL 3.0 hosts.
+ * No-op below 3.4 (EL8 builds against 1.1.1).
+ */
+#if OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER >= 0x30400000L
+#undef EVP_MD_CTX_size
+#define EVP_MD_CTX_size(ctx) EVP_MD_get_size(EVP_MD_CTX_get0_md(ctx))
+#endif
+
 
 #ifndef OPENSSL_THREADS
 #  error "OPENSSL_THREADS is not defined, Python requires thread-safe OpenSSL"

--- a/Modules/_ssl.c
+++ b/Modules/_ssl.c
@@ -4485,7 +4485,13 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
-#if OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER < 0x30300000L
+/*
+ * PERCONA: always use upstream's polyfill instead of the library's
+ * X509_STORE_get1_objects (OPENSSL_3.3.0 symbol version), so the module
+ * stays loadable on OpenSSL 3.0 hosts. Renamed to avoid clashing with
+ * the library declaration when building against >= 3.3 headers.
+ */
+#define X509_STORE_get1_objects _percona_X509_STORE_get1_objects
 static X509_OBJECT *x509_object_dup(const X509_OBJECT *obj)
 {
     int ok;
@@ -4527,7 +4533,6 @@
     X509_STORE_unlock(store);
     return ret;
 }
-#endif
 
 PyDoc_STRVAR(PySSLContext_sni_callback_doc,
 "Set a callback that will be called when a server name is provided by the SSL/TLS client in the SNI extension.\n\
